A nosebleed becomes an emergency when it is prolonged (lasting more than 20 minutes), heavy, or associated with symptoms like shortness of breath, chest pain, rapid bleeding, or altered mental status. Severe or recurrent nosebleeds in individuals with bleeding disorders or those on anticoagulant medications also require immediate medical attention. Furthermore, if a child experiences a nosebleed, it's crucial to seek help as their smaller airways may quickly become compromised.
